  "text": " step by step:\n\n1. Let $x = \\sqrt[3]{a} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}.$\n2. Then, $x^3 = (\\sqrt[3]{a} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}})^3 = a + \\frac{1}{a} + 3\\sqrt[3]{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}(\\sqrt[3]{a} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{a}}) = a + \\frac{1}{a} + 3(x).$\n3. Simplifying, we get $x^3 = a + \\frac{1}{a} + 3x.$\n4. Since $a^3 - 3a^2 - 8a + 13 = 0,$ we can divide by $a^3$ to get $1 - \\frac{3}{a} - \\frac{8}{a^2} + \\frac{13}{a^3} = 0.$\n5. Multiplying by $a^3,$ we get $a^3 - 3a^2 - 8a + 13 = 0.$\n6. Therefore, $a + \\frac{1}{a} = x^3 - 3x.$\n7. Substituting this back into the equation from step 3, we get $x^3 = x^3 - 3x + 3x = x^3.$\n8. Solving for $x,$ we get $x = \\boxed{2}.$",
